<Base
  title="How This Site Is Built"
  description="Two tracks with a fallback rule for the switch, Playwright tests that read the site as a visitor does, a portability scan, a dev container, and a pinned pipeline."
  track="technical"
  counterpart="/#about"
>
  <h1>How This Site Is Built</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A static Astro site, built with an AI coding agent, under the same rules as the rest of the laboratory:
    small steps, evidence for every claim, and checks that sit outside the agent.
  </p>

  <h2>Two Tracks, One Switch</h2>
  <p>
    Every page belongs to a track: the plain surface, or the technical deep end. Each page names where its
    switch goes. If the page has a twin on the other track, the switch goes there. If it doesn't, the switch
    goes to the section of the other track that its content belongs to, so neither track carries filler pages
    written only to be switched to. This page is an example: it has no plain twin, so its switch goes to the
    About section on the surface.
  </p>
  <p>
    Case studies are a content collection with a schema; each plain story names its technical twin in its
    frontmatter. The switch animates with the View Transitions API: down a layer when digging deeper, back up
    when surfacing, and no motion at all for readers who ask for reduced motion.
  </p>

  <h2>Tests That Look At The Site Like A Visitor</h2>
  <p>
    The tests were written before the pages they test. Playwright builds and serves the site, then, on a
    desktop and a phone-sized browser:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>crawls every internal link from the home page, and checks each page loads, is described, states its licenses, and has a switch that lands on the other track;</li>
    <li>checks an unknown address returns the custom 404 page, with a 404 status;</li>
    <li>checks a shared link carries a title, a description, and a 1200 by 630 preview image that actually loads;</li>
    <li>clicks the switch down and back, taking screenshots on the way;</li>
    <li>scans the built HTML for anything that must not be published: work item keys, identifiers, email addresses, money, machine names.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The preview image itself is rendered by the same browser, from an HTML card, so there's no image editor in
    the toolchain.
  </p>

  <h2>A Dev Container</h2>
  <p>
    The toolchain is declared in the repository rather than assumed from the machine: Node.js 24, the same
    version the pipeline uses, the Astro and Playwright editor extensions, and the dev server's port. Package
    caches live inside the container, not in my home directory.
  </p>

  <h2>The Pipeline</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Only GitHub's own actions run, each pinned to a full commit SHA with its version in a comment; the repository's settings refuse anything else.</li>
    <li>The tests run in Playwright's own container image, so the browser and its libraries are not installed on every run.</li>
    <li>Dependabot proposes updates, after a three-day cooldown for new releases.</li>
    <li>A pull request can merge only when the build and the tests pass.</li>
    <li>Merge commits only: GitHub signs its own merge commits, so the main line stays verifiably signed. Rebase merging would drop the signatures.</li>
  </ul>

